          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Analog Electromechanical Indicating Instruments: PMMC, Moving Iron, and Electrodynamometer",
          "content": "# Unit 2 \u2014 DC meters and resistance measurement\n\n**Why this unit matters.** Model an instrument as a circuit element with finite impedance and a bounded accuracy specification.\n\n**Application setting.** A design study for low-voltage measurements in a supervised teaching laboratory. The examples are synthetic, deliberately low-energy, and do not establish safe procedures for handling energized high-power systems.\n\n## Concept map\n\nEvery test instrument is part of an electrical system. A voltmeter presents finite input impedance; an ammeter introduces series burden; resistance tests inject excitation; probes add capacitance. The influence of the meter is largest when its equivalent impedance is not negligible relative to the measured circuit. It is useful to draw two schematic circuits: the original network and the network with the instrument installed.\n\nProbe intrusion is not confined to changed voltage. Test currents dissipate I\u00b2R, may change the resistance of temperature-sensitive devices and may alter semiconductor operating points. The source then no longer obeys the same ideal linear model. A four-wire geometry removes nominal lead drops from the sensed voltage but does not remove self-heating. Record current polarity and duty cycle for low-resistance tests.\n\n## Lessons and reading sequence\n\n- **Lesson 2.1** \u2014 [DMM accuracy, resolution and counts](../lessons/U02-L01-dmm-accuracy-resolution-and-counts.md)\n- **Lesson 2.2** \u2014 [Input resistance and voltage-divider loading](../lessons/U02-L02-input-resistance-and-voltage-divider-loading.md)\n- **Lesson 2.3** \u2014 [Two-wire versus Kelvin four-wire resistance](../lessons/U02-L03-two-wire-versus-kelvin-four-wire-resistance.md)\n- **Lesson 2.4** \u2014 [Current shunts and burden voltage](../lessons/U02-L04-current-shunts-and-burden-voltage.md)\n\nAfter reading the lessons, complete the [unit assessment](../assessments/unit-02.html), [research-style project](../projects/unit-02.html), and [interactive software model](../simulations/model-02.html). Follow the sequence for conceptual continuity, but revisit earlier examples when error analysis changes the meaning of a later result.\n\n## Five essential terms\n\n- **Input impedance:** Impedance seen by circuit at the measurement terminals.\n- **Burden:** Additional voltage or impedance introduced by current sensing.\n- **Kelvin connection:** Separate force and sense terminals.\n- **Resolution:** Smallest representable step under a defined mode.\n- **Accuracy specification:** Conditional bound or performance statement.\n\n## Cross-lesson example: competing explanations\n\nAssume two engineers report conflicting results for the same notional specimen. One engineer repeats a measurement ten times and obtains a very narrow cluster. The second uses another range, reference, sample timing or wiring topology and obtains a systematic difference. The first engineer's repeatability does not prove that the second engineer is wrong. In this unit, list the physical differences between instrument chains and predict which could create a reproducible offset or scale change. Identify one controlled experiment that would separate the causes without relying on a higher number of decimal places.\n\n## Unit laboratory notebook template\n\nUse fields for measurand, connection topology, symbol definitions, numeric input sources, model assumptions, instrument settings, raw observations, rejection criteria, derived values, uncertainty budget, limitations and conclusion. Include a citation for each manufacturer specification if a real instrument is discussed. Reserve a separate field for software version and simulator parameters; a teaching simulator is not a calibrated artifact.\n\n## Reader checkpoint\n\n1. Explain the difference between an instrument's reported indication and the intended measurand.\n2. Give one case in which making the display resolution finer does not improve the measurement result.\n3. Identify one quantity in the unit simulator that must be experimentally calibrated before using the equation for a real system.\n4. Define the most important limitation of the idealized model and a practical observation that could falsify it.\n\n**Answer guidance.** A sound response links the observed quantity to the physical chain and names a bias or uncertainty source instead of vaguely saying 'human error.' It distinguishes a model prediction from independent evidence. Complete safety qualification is outside the scope of this book.\n\n**Safety note:** The examples are synthetic and intended for analysis or software-only simulation. Do not connect measuring instruments to mains, high-energy equipment, CT secondaries or exposed conductors without authorized training, appropriately rated equipment and site-specific procedures. Instrument-category markings, isolation ratings and current limits must be verified in the manufacturer documentation.\n"
