          "heading": "Engineering Design Project & Numerical Investigation: Instrument Extension: Ammeter Shunts, Voltmeter Multipliers, and Loading Errors",
          "content": "# Project 3 \u2014 Bridge circuits and impedance measurements: reproducible measurement review\n\n**Research question.** Can two plausible measurement chains give meaningfully different estimates of the same physical measurand when one ideal-model assumption is violated?\n\n**Starting case.** A low-energy standards-comparison exercise with no energized utility apparatus. This is a software-only project. It must not be implemented by handling energized installations or using the simulation as a substitute for calibration.\n\n## Background and model selection\n\nNull measurement transforms the problem into a comparison of ratios. Sensitivity still depends on excitation, component tolerances and the slope of bridge output with the unknown parameter. Balance often reduces detector calibration dependence while increasing the need to control fixture leakage, thermoelectric voltages and temperature gradients. For AC bridges, complex balance requires both amplitude and phase conditions.\n\nAt high frequency, a lead contains distributed capacitance and inductance. At very low currents, leakage resistance can be of the same order as the device under test. In both extremes the fixture may dominate. Model the connection explicitly, choose a frequency sweep or guarded arrangement, then report whether compensation and fixture repeatability were actually checked.\n\nSelect one formula from each of Lessons 3.1\u20133.4, but model one of them in depth. For the chosen case, clearly define independent input, measured indication, desired output, units, reference conditions and primary sensitivity. Explain why changing a software parameter is a model intervention, not a laboratory measurement.\n\n## Task A \u2014 baseline and synthetic evidence\n\nOpen [Unit 3 simulator](../simulations/model-03.html). Save the four slider values, the computed numerical output, the axes and the model description. Change one input to three different values and record the response. Independently calculate one point. Create a small table including percent deviations from the baseline and at least one limiting-case check. Where a plotted variable is not the same as an instrument's recorded output, document that distinction.\n\n## Task B \u2014 deliberately introduce error\n\nChoose one missing mechanism from the lesson limitations. The candidates specific to this unit include: Self-heating and resistor temperature coefficients can shift apparent equilibrium; a detector with low impedance perturbs the bridge. Another candidate is: Frequency-dependent winding resistance and core loss can shift actual phase; series and parallel equivalent forms differ with loss and frequency. Write a small correction term, state its assumed magnitude and investigate how the inferred result changes. Do not assert that an invented error magnitude is representative of a commercial instrument or a deployed installation.\n\n## Task C \u2014 contrast two explanations\n\nTwo technical reports disagree. One report blames calibration; the other blames a physical effect: **A short lead at one frequency may be a significant inductor at a much higher frequency.** Design a low-energy or observational check that would produce different predictions under those explanations. State the independent evidence source and explain how correlated calibration references could defeat the check. If both explanations predict the same observable under your selected settings, your proposed test is not discriminating and must be redesigned.\n\n## Task D \u2014 quantitative uncertainty and decision\n\nAssign three clearly labeled synthetic standard uncertainties and propagate them through the selected measurement equation. Where appropriate, include a common-reference correlation. Present a result estimate, combined standard uncertainty and an optional expanded uncertainty with specified k. Introduce a fictional tolerance limit and a predeclared decision rule. Show a near-boundary example where the displayed reading by itself would give a different decision from a guard-banded approach. Do not conflate a rejected acceptance decision with proof of nonconformity.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- Reproducible spreadsheet or notebook with units, formulas and a synthetic-data flag.\n- At least one parameter-sensitivity plot and one tabulated calculation.\n- A short model-assumption statement and a distinguishable rival hypothesis.\n- A traceable uncertainty budget with corrections separated from uncertainty contributions.\n- A final one-page technical memo explaining what is supported and what remains unmeasured.\n\n## Evaluation and extension\n\nA report is complete only when a reader can reproduce the model calculation, recognize synthetic inputs and identify the decisive missing evidence for a real-world conclusion. Additional complexity earns credit only if it addresses the dominant uncertainty, not simply because the diagram becomes more elaborate. For an extension, estimate how much improved reference accuracy or increased acquisition time would actually reduce the dominant combined uncertainty under the stated model.\n\n**Safety note:** The examples are synthetic and intended for analysis or software-only simulation. Do not connect measuring instruments to mains, high-energy equipment, CT secondaries or exposed conductors without authorized training, appropriately rated equipment and site-specific procedures. Instrument-category markings, isolation ratings and current limits must be verified in the manufacturer documentation.\n"
