          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Direct-Current Potentiometers and Precision Low-Resistance Kelvin Bridges",
          "content": "# Unit 4 \u2014 AC power, energy and instrument transformers\n\n**Why this unit matters.** Keep RMS, average, apparent power, active power, phase error and harmonic content separate.\n\n**Application setting.** An analytical energy-audit scenario using fictional low-voltage waveforms. The examples are synthetic, deliberately low-energy, and do not establish safe procedures for handling energized high-power systems.\n\n## Concept map\n\nA time-domain power meter calculates the average of instantaneous p(t)=v(t)i(t). For sinusoids, this reduces to a phase-based expression. For harmonics, active power contributions depend on the matching harmonic components of voltage and current; simply multiplying totals by a single cosine can be misleading. Sampling alignment, anti-alias filtering and sensor phase delay matter for real power.\n\nCurrent-transformer accuracy depends on magnitude, frequency, waveform and secondary burden. A ratio printed on a nameplate is an ideal scaling relationship, not a complete model of phase displacement. This topic is safety-critical: software demonstrations must not be mistaken for procedures for opening or rewiring an installed CT secondary.\n\n## Lessons and reading sequence\n\n- **Lesson 4.1** \u2014 [RMS, average, crest factor and distortion](../lessons/U04-L01-rms-average-crest-factor-and-distortion.md)\n- **Lesson 4.2** \u2014 [Real, reactive and apparent power](../lessons/U04-L02-real-reactive-and-apparent-power.md)\n- **Lesson 4.3** \u2014 [Three-phase power and two-wattmeter method](../lessons/U04-L03-three-phase-power-and-two-wattmeter-method.md)\n- **Lesson 4.4** \u2014 [Instrument transformers, ratio and phase errors](../lessons/U04-L04-instrument-transformers-ratio-and-phase-errors.md)\n\nAfter reading the lessons, complete the [unit assessment](../assessments/unit-04.html), [research-style project](../projects/unit-04.html), and [interactive software model](../simulations/model-04.html). Follow the sequence for conceptual continuity, but revisit earlier examples when error analysis changes the meaning of a later result.\n\n## Five essential terms\n\n- **RMS:** Square root of mean squared instantaneous waveform.\n- **Power factor:** Ratio of active to apparent power in appropriate definitions.\n- **Burden VA:** Secondary apparent power delivered to transformer load.\n- **Crest factor:** Peak magnitude divided by RMS magnitude.\n- **Phase displacement:** Angular error between reference and measured waveforms.\n\n## Cross-lesson example: competing explanations\n\nAssume two engineers report conflicting results for the same notional specimen. One engineer repeats a measurement ten times and obtains a very narrow cluster. The second uses another range, reference, sample timing or wiring topology and obtains a systematic difference. The first engineer's repeatability does not prove that the second engineer is wrong. In this unit, list the physical differences between instrument chains and predict which could create a reproducible offset or scale change. Identify one controlled experiment that would separate the causes without relying on a higher number of decimal places.\n\n## Unit laboratory notebook template\n\nUse fields for measurand, connection topology, symbol definitions, numeric input sources, model assumptions, instrument settings, raw observations, rejection criteria, derived values, uncertainty budget, limitations and conclusion. Include a citation for each manufacturer specification if a real instrument is discussed. Reserve a separate field for software version and simulator parameters; a teaching simulator is not a calibrated artifact.\n\n## Reader checkpoint\n\n1. Explain the difference between an instrument's reported indication and the intended measurand.\n2. Give one case in which making the display resolution finer does not improve the measurement result.\n3. Identify one quantity in the unit simulator that must be experimentally calibrated before using the equation for a real system.\n4. Define the most important limitation of the idealized model and a practical observation that could falsify it.\n\n**Answer guidance.** A sound response links the observed quantity to the physical chain and names a bias or uncertainty source instead of vaguely saying 'human error.' It distinguishes a model prediction from independent evidence. Complete safety qualification is outside the scope of this book.\n\n**Safety note:** The examples are synthetic and intended for analysis or software-only simulation. Do not connect measuring instruments to mains, high-energy equipment, CT secondaries or exposed conductors without authorized training, appropriately rated equipment and site-specific procedures. Instrument-category markings, isolation ratings and current limits must be verified in the manufacturer documentation.\n"
