          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Power and Energy Measurement: Single-Phase and Polyphase Electrodynamic Wattmeters",
          "content": "# Unit 6 \u2014 ADCs, references and data acquisition\n\n**Why this unit matters.** Connect converter architecture, voltage reference, sampling and digital processing to a reported number.\n\n**Application setting.** Design a synthetic acquisition system and document what the digital numbers mean. The examples are synthetic, deliberately low-energy, and do not establish safe procedures for handling energized high-power systems.\n\n## Concept map\n\nConverting input voltage to a code involves a reference, sampling capacitor, comparator or integrator, clock and digital processing. The ideal LSB quantifies nominal granularity only. A high-bit count can coexist with offset, reference, settling, clock jitter and INL errors. A performance claim must name the analysis bandwidth, input frequency and operating temperature.\n\nDistributed observations require a consistent timebase. Channels sampled sequentially carry physically different observation instants even when displayed as a simultaneous table row. For an oscillatory signal, a microsecond-level skew may map into a large apparent phase shift at kilohertz frequencies. Preserve hardware sample times or characterize skew before computing cross-channel products.\n\n## Lessons and reading sequence\n\n- **Lesson 6.1** \u2014 [ADC quantization and transfer functions](../lessons/U06-L01-adc-quantization-and-transfer-functions.md)\n- **Lesson 6.2** \u2014 [Sample-and-hold settling and reference integrity](../lessons/U06-L02-sample-and-hold-settling-and-reference-integrity.md)\n- **Lesson 6.3** \u2014 [SNR, SINAD, ENOB and oversampling](../lessons/U06-L03-snr-sinad-enob-and-oversampling.md)\n- **Lesson 6.4** \u2014 [Timestamping, multiplexing and synchronized acquisition](../lessons/U06-L04-timestamping-multiplexing-and-synchronized-acquisition.md)\n\nAfter reading the lessons, complete the [unit assessment](../assessments/unit-06.html), [research-style project](../projects/unit-06.html), and [interactive software model](../simulations/model-06.html). Follow the sequence for conceptual continuity, but revisit earlier examples when error analysis changes the meaning of a later result.\n\n## Five essential terms\n\n- **LSB:** Nominal least significant code step.\n- **ENOB:** Equivalent resolution estimated from SINAD.\n- **Settling:** Convergence toward a stable value after a change.\n- **Skew:** Difference between channel sampling instants.\n- **INL:** Deviation of ADC transfer from an ideal reference line.\n\n## Cross-lesson example: competing explanations\n\nAssume two engineers report conflicting results for the same notional specimen. One engineer repeats a measurement ten times and obtains a very narrow cluster. The second uses another range, reference, sample timing or wiring topology and obtains a systematic difference. The first engineer's repeatability does not prove that the second engineer is wrong. In this unit, list the physical differences between instrument chains and predict which could create a reproducible offset or scale change. Identify one controlled experiment that would separate the causes without relying on a higher number of decimal places.\n\n## Unit laboratory notebook template\n\nUse fields for measurand, connection topology, symbol definitions, numeric input sources, model assumptions, instrument settings, raw observations, rejection criteria, derived values, uncertainty budget, limitations and conclusion. Include a citation for each manufacturer specification if a real instrument is discussed. Reserve a separate field for software version and simulator parameters; a teaching simulator is not a calibrated artifact.\n\n## Reader checkpoint\n\n1. Explain the difference between an instrument's reported indication and the intended measurand.\n2. Give one case in which making the display resolution finer does not improve the measurement result.\n3. Identify one quantity in the unit simulator that must be experimentally calibrated before using the equation for a real system.\n4. Define the most important limitation of the idealized model and a practical observation that could falsify it.\n\n**Answer guidance.** A sound response links the observed quantity to the physical chain and names a bias or uncertainty source instead of vaguely saying 'human error.' It distinguishes a model prediction from independent evidence. Complete safety qualification is outside the scope of this book.\n\n**Safety note:** The examples are synthetic and intended for analysis or software-only simulation. Do not connect measuring instruments to mains, high-energy equipment, CT secondaries or exposed conductors without authorized training, appropriately rated equipment and site-specific procedures. Instrument-category markings, isolation ratings and current limits must be verified in the manufacturer documentation.\n"
