          "heading": "Engineering Design Project & Numerical Investigation: Instrument Transformers: Current Transformers (CT), Potential Transformers (PT), and Phase Errors",
          "content": "# Project 7 \u2014 Sensors, signal conditioning and calibration: reproducible measurement review\n\n**Research question.** Can two plausible measurement chains give meaningfully different estimates of the same physical measurand when one ideal-model assumption is violated?\n\n**Starting case.** Build mathematical calibration curves before relying on a sensor as evidence. This is a software-only project. It must not be implemented by handling energized installations or using the simulation as a substitute for calibration.\n\n## Background and model selection\n\nA sensor is not identical to its electrical output. It is a chain from physical measurand to material response, transducer, excitation, wiring, amplifier and digitizer. Temperature, stress direction, spatial averaging and mounting create biases that cannot be removed by digital arithmetic alone. Calibration establishes a relation over a specified range rather than proving every physical mechanism understood.\n\nA small sum of squared residuals is not enough. Test independent inputs, rising and falling trajectories, different environmental conditions and repeated runs. Compare interpolations against extrapolations. Hysteresis or creep means that the same instantaneous input can produce multiple outputs depending on history, defeating an unconditional one-to-one calibration curve.\n\nSelect one formula from each of Lessons 7.1\u20137.4, but model one of them in depth. For the chosen case, clearly define independent input, measured indication, desired output, units, reference conditions and primary sensitivity. Explain why changing a software parameter is a model intervention, not a laboratory measurement.\n\n## Task A \u2014 baseline and synthetic evidence\n\nOpen [Unit 7 simulator](../simulations/model-07.html). Save the four slider values, the computed numerical output, the axes and the model description. Change one input to three different values and record the response. Independently calculate one point. Create a small table including percent deviations from the baseline and at least one limiting-case check. Where a plotted variable is not the same as an instrument's recorded output, document that distinction.\n\n## Task B \u2014 deliberately introduce error\n\nChoose one missing mechanism from the lesson limitations. The candidates specific to this unit include: Nonuniform strain, adhesive creep, transverse sensitivity and thermal expansion affect physical interpretation. Another candidate is: Actual calibrated RTD curves use more complete relationships; the linear formula is a restricted teaching approximation. Write a small correction term, state its assumed magnitude and investigate how the inferred result changes. Do not assert that an invented error magnitude is representative of a commercial instrument or a deployed installation.\n\n## Task C \u2014 contrast two explanations\n\nTwo technical reports disagree. One report blames calibration; the other blames a physical effect: **Temperature-driven offset shifts can look like a small real DC current.** Design a low-energy or observational check that would produce different predictions under those explanations. State the independent evidence source and explain how correlated calibration references could defeat the check. If both explanations predict the same observable under your selected settings, your proposed test is not discriminating and must be redesigned.\n\n## Task D \u2014 quantitative uncertainty and decision\n\nAssign three clearly labeled synthetic standard uncertainties and propagate them through the selected measurement equation. Where appropriate, include a common-reference correlation. Present a result estimate, combined standard uncertainty and an optional expanded uncertainty with specified k. Introduce a fictional tolerance limit and a predeclared decision rule. Show a near-boundary example where the displayed reading by itself would give a different decision from a guard-banded approach. Do not conflate a rejected acceptance decision with proof of nonconformity.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- Reproducible spreadsheet or notebook with units, formulas and a synthetic-data flag.\n- At least one parameter-sensitivity plot and one tabulated calculation.\n- A short model-assumption statement and a distinguishable rival hypothesis.\n- A traceable uncertainty budget with corrections separated from uncertainty contributions.\n- A final one-page technical memo explaining what is supported and what remains unmeasured.\n\n## Evaluation and extension\n\nA report is complete only when a reader can reproduce the model calculation, recognize synthetic inputs and identify the decisive missing evidence for a real-world conclusion. Additional complexity earns credit only if it addresses the dominant uncertainty, not simply because the diagram becomes more elaborate. For an extension, estimate how much improved reference accuracy or increased acquisition time would actually reduce the dominant combined uncertainty under the stated model.\n\n**Safety note:** The examples are synthetic and intended for analysis or software-only simulation. Do not connect measuring instruments to mains, high-energy equipment, CT secondaries or exposed conductors without authorized training, appropriately rated equipment and site-specific procedures. Instrument-category markings, isolation ratings and current limits must be verified in the manufacturer documentation.\n"
